div.p:
There are several points which could be made to the Chinese:
—we could officially call their attention to the changes in our import control and passport regulations;
—we could call to their attention the reduction or removal of the destroyer patrol in the Taiwan Straits;
—we could call to their attention the statement by Elliot Richardson on the Sino-Soviet problem, and expand somewhat on the theme of our position of non-collaboration with the Soviets;
—we could note the formulation quoted above, on “normal relations” despite differences of principle, and inquire whether this could apply in our relationship;
—finally we could expand on the strategic implications for Peking of the President’s Vietnam policy:
a.
we are not threatening China; indeed we are trying to end the war and are withdrawing troops from both Vietnam and Thailand; b.
we have not sought to take advantage of Chinese problems on the Soviet border; c.
that peace in Southeast Asia would open up new possibilities in our relations with Peking, along the lines of the President’s backgrounder in Guam, etc.
